WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.010
Imagine your client can use the same agents you use.

2
00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:03.690
They can build an automation themselves.

3
00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.290
The agent can program for them.

4
00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.740
So why would they keep paying you?

5
00:00:07.037 --> 00:00:08.567
That’s the question many people who work

6
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:09.967
in automation, agents,

7
00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:12.467
consulting, technology, or management still aren’t asking

8
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:13.007
themselves.

9
00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:13.987
And I think it’s one of the

10
00:00:13.987 --> 00:00:15.707
most important changes happening right now.

11
00:00:15.707 --> 00:00:16.947
So let’s take it step by step.

12
00:00:16.947 --> 00:00:17.967
In recent years,

13
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:19.267
people who got into this market early

14
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:20.507
had two big advantages.

15
00:00:20.507 --> 00:00:21.627
The first was technical.

16
00:00:21.627 --> 00:00:23.507
You knew how to connect an API.

17
00:00:23.507 --> 00:00:24.887
Configure an agent.

18
00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:26.767
Connect the CRM to WhatsApp.

19
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:27.967
Work with webhooks,

20
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:29.847
databases, servers, VPSs.

21
00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:31.547
And make it all work reliably enough.

22
00:00:31.547 --> 00:00:32.587
The client didn’t know how.

23
00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:33.387
So they needed you.

24
00:00:33.387 --> 00:00:35.007
But there was a second advantage,

25
00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:36.387
an even bigger one.

26
00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:37.727
You knew a lot more about artificial

27
00:00:37.727 --> 00:00:39.447
intelligence than your client did.

28
00:00:39.447 --> 00:00:40.887
You studied AI every day,

29
00:00:40.887 --> 00:00:42.547
while the business owner was still asking,

30
00:00:42.547 --> 00:00:45.047
“But can ChatGPT do that?” That difference

31
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:46.027
in knowledge was worth money.

32
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:47.807
The bigger the gap between what you

33
00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:49.287
knew and what your client knew,

34
00:00:49.287 --> 00:00:50.687
the more value you delivered.

35
00:00:50.687 --> 00:00:52.067
And the more you could charge.

36
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:54.047
The problem is that both advantages are

37
00:00:54.047 --> 00:00:55.487
changing at the same time.

38
00:00:55.487 --> 00:00:56.547
The first change:

39
00:00:56.547 --> 00:00:58.667
the technical barrier is coming down.

40
00:00:58.667 --> 00:00:59.707
Not long ago,

41
00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:02.167
automating a business required someone who understood

42
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:04.427
API documentation, authentication,

43
00:01:04.427 --> 00:01:06.287
infrastructure, integrations,

44
00:01:06.287 --> 00:01:07.327
and databases.

45
00:01:07.327 --> 00:01:09.267
Today, you go to an agent and

46
00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:11.287
say, “Analyze this process and build a

47
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:12.827
solution.” And it gets started.

48
00:01:12.827 --> 00:01:13.927
It researches.

49
00:01:13.927 --> 00:01:14.807
Programs.

50
00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:15.727
Tests.

51
00:01:15.727 --> 00:01:16.367
Fixes.

52
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:16.907
Integrates.

53
00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:18.847
And each new generation of models does

54
00:01:18.847 --> 00:01:19.847
this better than the last.

55
00:01:19.847 --> 00:01:21.487
No, it doesn’t all work perfectly yet.

56
00:01:21.487 --> 00:01:23.007
But look at the direction things are

57
00:01:23.007 --> 00:01:23.327
going.

58
00:01:23.327 --> 00:01:24.847
The technical side is getting cheaper,

59
00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:26.147
faster, and, above all,

60
00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:26.867
more accessible.

61
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:28.567
The second change is even more important.

62
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:29.927
Your client is learning too.

63
00:01:29.927 --> 00:01:31.647
Today, you probably still know a lot

64
00:01:31.647 --> 00:01:33.627
more about AI than most business owners.

65
00:01:33.627 --> 00:01:35.247
But that gap is shrinking fast.

66
00:01:35.247 --> 00:01:36.807
They already use ChatGPT.

67
00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:37.867
They’ve tried Claude.

68
00:01:37.867 --> 00:01:39.167
They’ve done deep research,

69
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:40.187
generated an image,

70
00:01:40.187 --> 00:01:41.207
maybe even a video.

71
00:01:41.207 --> 00:01:43.127
Before long, they’ll be using agents inside

72
00:01:43.127 --> 00:01:44.047
their own businesses.

73
00:01:44.047 --> 00:01:46.287
And it’s quite possible that by 2027,

74
00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:48.487
the difference between what you know and

75
00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:50.167
what your client knows will be much

76
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:50.647
smaller.

77
00:01:50.647 --> 00:01:51.327
Not gone.

78
00:01:51.327 --> 00:01:52.367
But much smaller.

79
00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:54.187
And then that uncomfortable question from the

80
00:01:54.187 --> 00:01:55.147
beginning comes back:

81
00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:56.267
if they can do it too,

82
00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:57.567
why would they pay you?

83
00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:58.627
This isn’t new.

84
00:01:58.627 --> 00:01:59.907
It’s happened before.

85
00:01:59.907 --> 00:02:01.507
Think about building websites.

86
00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:04.407
Years ago, making a website required HTML,

87
00:02:04.407 --> 00:02:05.607
CSS,

88
00:02:06.087 --> 00:02:07.267
a server, hosting,

89
00:02:07.267 --> 00:02:08.967
DNS, and programming.

90
00:02:08.967 --> 00:02:10.287
If you knew how to do it,

91
00:02:10.287 --> 00:02:11.327
you had a rare skill.

92
00:02:11.327 --> 00:02:12.747
Then WordPress, Wix,

93
00:02:12.747 --> 00:02:14.227
and many other tools came along.

94
00:02:14.227 --> 00:02:16.107
Today, practically anyone can put a website

95
00:02:16.107 --> 00:02:16.727
online.

96
00:02:16.727 --> 00:02:18.467
Did the website market disappear?

97
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:19.147
No.

98
00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:21.247
But making a simple website stopped being

99
00:02:21.247 --> 00:02:21.987
extraordinary.

100
00:02:21.987 --> 00:02:23.227
It became a commodity.

101
00:02:23.227 --> 00:02:24.787
And who kept making good money?

102
00:02:24.787 --> 00:02:26.307
People who got really good at something

103
00:02:26.307 --> 00:02:26.767
else.

104
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:27.347
Design.

105
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:28.087
Branding.

106
00:02:28.087 --> 00:02:28.847
Conversion.

107
00:02:28.847 --> 00:02:29.507
Sales.

108
00:02:29.507 --> 00:02:31.087
Deep knowledge of an industry.

109
00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:32.947
Technology became just one part of what

110
00:02:32.947 --> 00:02:33.567
they delivered.

111
00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:35.127
I believe the same thing is happening

112
00:02:35.127 --> 00:02:36.147
now with agents.

113
00:02:36.147 --> 00:02:37.307
And here’s a point I think is

114
00:02:37.307 --> 00:02:37.807
essential.

115
00:02:37.807 --> 00:02:39.207
The problem isn’t that AI is getting

116
00:02:39.207 --> 00:02:39.647
better.

117
00:02:39.647 --> 00:02:41.247
The problem is staying the same while

118
00:02:41.247 --> 00:02:42.107
AI improves.

119
00:02:42.107 --> 00:02:43.187
Picture a staircase.

120
00:02:43.187 --> 00:02:44.707
You’re a few steps above the average

121
00:02:44.707 --> 00:02:45.587
person.

122
00:02:45.587 --> 00:02:46.667
There’s a good gap.

123
00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:48.307
But there’s a floor on that staircase.

124
00:02:48.307 --> 00:02:50.127
And the floor is rising.

125
00:02:50.127 --> 00:02:51.147
Models improve.

126
00:02:51.147 --> 00:02:52.247
Tools improve.

127
00:02:52.247 --> 00:02:53.187
People learn.

128
00:02:53.187 --> 00:02:54.887
If you stay on the same step,

129
00:02:54.887 --> 00:02:56.587
you haven’t technically gotten worse.

130
00:02:56.587 --> 00:02:58.087
But compared with the market,

131
00:02:58.087 --> 00:02:59.247
you’ve fallen behind.

132
00:02:59.247 --> 00:03:00.707
That’s why it’s not enough to keep

133
00:03:00.707 --> 00:03:01.107
learning.

134
00:03:01.107 --> 00:03:02.547
You need to learn faster than the

135
00:03:02.547 --> 00:03:03.407
floor rises.

136
00:03:03.407 --> 00:03:04.977
Someone might ask:

137
00:03:04.977 --> 00:03:07.207
“Nei, does that mean working with automation

138
00:03:07.207 --> 00:03:08.587
and agents is going to lose its

139
00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.747
value?” I think exactly the opposite.

140
00:03:10.747 --> 00:03:13.167
This transformation isn’t happening only to people

141
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:14.267
who work with automation.

142
00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:15.827
It’s happening to accountants,

143
00:03:15.827 --> 00:03:17.607
lawyers, programmers,

144
00:03:17.607 --> 00:03:19.527
marketing, customer service,

145
00:03:19.527 --> 00:03:20.867
and HR.

146
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:22.527
Practically every kind of knowledge work is

147
00:03:22.527 --> 00:03:23.107
being affected.

148
00:03:23.107 --> 00:03:24.567
So if there’s a good industry to

149
00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:25.287
be in right now,

150
00:03:25.287 --> 00:03:27.327
it’s the one making this transformation happen.

151
00:03:27.327 --> 00:03:29.007
We’re building the systems that will automate

152
00:03:29.007 --> 00:03:30.407
part of the work in every other

153
00:03:30.407 --> 00:03:30.827
field.

154
00:03:30.827 --> 00:03:32.207
The market is still enormous.

155
00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:34.380
What changes is the work we’ll do.

156
00:03:34.387 --> 00:03:35.507
And this is where the most important

157
00:03:35.507 --> 00:03:36.647
part of this video begins.

158
00:03:36.647 --> 00:03:38.307
Until now, a lot of people made

159
00:03:38.307 --> 00:03:38.947
money by saying,

160
00:03:38.947 --> 00:03:41.167
“I’ll build your automation.” “I’ll create your

161
00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:43.707
agent.” “I’ll connect your systems.” That work

162
00:03:43.707 --> 00:03:44.527
will still exist.

163
00:03:44.527 --> 00:03:45.567
But more and more,

164
00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.027
the agent itself will do a lot

165
00:03:47.027 --> 00:03:47.567
of that building.

166
00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:48.947
So value moves up a level.

167
00:03:48.947 --> 00:03:50.107
The question shifts from,

168
00:03:50.107 --> 00:03:51.467
“Can you build it?” to,

169
00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:53.167
“What should we build?” It sounds like

170
00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:53.827
a small change.

171
00:03:53.827 --> 00:03:54.447
It’s huge.

172
00:03:54.447 --> 00:03:55.947
Because in a world where almost anything

173
00:03:55.947 --> 00:03:56.987
can be built quickly,

174
00:03:56.987 --> 00:03:59.327
building stops being the bottleneck.

175
00:03:59.327 --> 00:04:00.667
Deciding becomes the bottleneck.

176
00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:02.087
Which problem is worth solving?

177
00:04:02.087 --> 00:04:03.707
Which process should we change first?

178
00:04:03.707 --> 00:04:04.527
Where’s the waste?

179
00:04:04.527 --> 00:04:05.487
Where’s the opportunity?

180
00:04:05.487 --> 00:04:06.707
What should a person handle,

181
00:04:06.707 --> 00:04:08.047
what should go to an agent,

182
00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.227
and what should remain a traditional automation?

183
00:04:10.227 --> 00:04:11.607
Where should the person and the agent

184
00:04:11.607 --> 00:04:12.187
work together?

185
00:04:12.187 --> 00:04:14.227
And what activity should simply disappear?

186
00:04:14.227 --> 00:04:15.427
There’s a name for that.

187
00:04:15.427 --> 00:04:16.347
It’s management.

188
00:04:16.347 --> 00:04:17.507
And that’s why I’ve been saying for

189
00:04:17.507 --> 00:04:18.067
a long time:

190
00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:19.527
if you know how to manage people,

191
00:04:19.527 --> 00:04:20.967
you have a huge advantage in learning

192
00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:21.807
to manage agents.

193
00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:23.167
Think about what a good manager needs

194
00:04:23.167 --> 00:04:23.547
to know.

195
00:04:23.547 --> 00:04:24.287
What’s the goal?

196
00:04:24.287 --> 00:04:25.287
What are the priorities?

197
00:04:25.287 --> 00:04:25.987
Who does what?

198
00:04:25.987 --> 00:04:27.587
What information does that person need,

199
00:04:27.587 --> 00:04:29.087
and what resources can they use?

200
00:04:29.087 --> 00:04:30.567
How much autonomy do they have?

201
00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:31.847
How do you measure the result,

202
00:04:31.847 --> 00:04:32.827
when do you step in,

203
00:04:32.827 --> 00:04:33.867
how do you give feedback,

204
00:04:33.867 --> 00:04:35.667
and how do you improve the process?

205
00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.587
Now replace the word person with agent.

206
00:04:37.587 --> 00:04:38.787
What’s the agent’s goal?

207
00:04:38.787 --> 00:04:40.367
What context does it get?

208
00:04:40.367 --> 00:04:41.687
What tools can it use?

209
00:04:41.687 --> 00:04:43.087
What can it do on its own,

210
00:04:43.087 --> 00:04:44.327
and what needs approval?

211
00:04:44.327 --> 00:04:46.367
How do we evaluate its response,

212
00:04:46.367 --> 00:04:47.427
where did it go wrong,

213
00:04:47.427 --> 00:04:48.987
and how can it improve tomorrow?

214
00:04:48.987 --> 00:04:50.407
It’s practically the same list.

215
00:04:50.407 --> 00:04:51.687
That’s agent management.

216
00:04:51.687 --> 00:04:53.007
And that’s where I see a huge

217
00:04:53.007 --> 00:04:54.587
mistake happening inside companies.

218
00:04:54.587 --> 00:04:55.687
Many managers think,

219
00:04:55.687 --> 00:04:57.687
“AI is for the tech team.” It

220
00:04:57.687 --> 00:04:58.247
isn’t.

221
00:04:58.247 --> 00:04:59.767
Programming may be technical.

222
00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:00.847
Infrastructure too.

223
00:05:00.847 --> 00:05:02.327
Specialized security, absolutely.

224
00:05:02.327 --> 00:05:05.247
But you can’t delegate understanding agents completely.

225
00:05:05.247 --> 00:05:07.287
Imagine a manager twenty years ago saying,

226
00:05:07.287 --> 00:05:08.547
“Computers are for IT.

227
00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:09.647
I don’t use email.

228
00:05:09.647 --> 00:05:11.247
My team handles it for me.” Or,

229
00:05:11.247 --> 00:05:13.587
“I don’t need to understand spreadsheets.” It

230
00:05:13.587 --> 00:05:14.927
would be impossible to run a modern

231
00:05:14.927 --> 00:05:15.747
business that way.

232
00:05:15.747 --> 00:05:17.487
The same thing will happen with agents.

233
00:05:17.487 --> 00:05:19.027
You don’t need to become a programmer.

234
00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:20.227
But you do need to learn to

235
00:05:20.227 --> 00:05:21.227
work with them directly.

236
00:05:21.227 --> 00:05:23.207
That’s exactly the thinking behind the content

237
00:05:23.207 --> 00:05:25.427
I’ve been creating at INEMA dot club.

238
00:05:25.427 --> 00:05:26.847
Take OSWork, for example.

239
00:05:26.847 --> 00:05:28.247
Some people look at it and ask,

240
00:05:28.247 --> 00:05:29.787
“Why does a manager need to open

241
00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:31.647
a terminal?” It’s not because I want

242
00:05:31.647 --> 00:05:34.127
to turn managers into Linux administrators.

243
00:05:34.307 --> 00:05:36.107
It’s because the terminal is becoming one

244
00:05:36.107 --> 00:05:37.527
of the most powerful ways to work

245
00:05:37.527 --> 00:05:38.187
with agents.

246
00:05:38.187 --> 00:05:39.467
You need to get over your fear

247
00:05:39.467 --> 00:05:40.287
of that environment.

248
00:05:40.287 --> 00:05:41.647
Understand the basics of folders,

249
00:05:41.647 --> 00:05:43.247
files, permissions, and a VPS.

250
00:05:43.247 --> 00:05:44.407
And understand agents,

251
00:05:44.407 --> 00:05:45.587
skills, context, memory,

252
00:05:45.587 --> 00:05:46.367
and tools.

253
00:05:46.367 --> 00:05:47.527
Not to become technical.

254
00:05:47.527 --> 00:05:48.667
To gain autonomy.

255
00:05:48.667 --> 00:05:50.307
That’s what we did when computers arrived.

256
00:05:50.307 --> 00:05:51.207
We learned the mouse,

257
00:05:51.207 --> 00:05:52.027
the keyboard, Windows.

258
00:05:52.027 --> 00:05:53.267
Then Word, spreadsheets,

259
00:05:53.267 --> 00:05:54.167
email, the browser.

260
00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:54.867
No one said,

261
00:05:54.867 --> 00:05:56.627
“I’m studying computer science because I learned

262
00:05:56.627 --> 00:05:58.307
Excel.” You were just learning to work

263
00:05:58.307 --> 00:05:58.987
in a new environment.

264
00:05:58.987 --> 00:06:00.887
Now we’re entering another environment.

265
00:06:00.887 --> 00:06:02.027
The agents’ environment.

266
00:06:02.027 --> 00:06:03.567
And there’s a huge window of opportunity

267
00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:04.167
right now.

268
00:06:04.167 --> 00:06:05.327
You can still learn at your own

269
00:06:05.327 --> 00:06:05.727
pace.

270
00:06:05.727 --> 00:06:07.587
You can create an agent and make

271
00:06:07.587 --> 00:06:08.087
a mistake.

272
00:06:08.087 --> 00:06:09.427
You can let it do something wrong

273
00:06:09.427 --> 00:06:10.687
and figure out why.

274
00:06:10.687 --> 00:06:12.087
You can improve the context,

275
00:06:12.087 --> 00:06:12.807
change a rule,

276
00:06:12.807 --> 00:06:13.687
create a skill,

277
00:06:13.687 --> 00:06:14.467
tear it all down,

278
00:06:14.467 --> 00:06:15.407
and start over.

279
00:06:15.407 --> 00:06:16.827
There’s still time to experiment.

280
00:06:16.827 --> 00:06:17.607
That’s a huge advantage.

281
00:06:17.607 --> 00:06:18.907
Because before long,

282
00:06:18.907 --> 00:06:20.307
you won’t be learning because you want

283
00:06:20.307 --> 00:06:20.527
to.

284
00:06:20.527 --> 00:06:21.927
You’ll learn because you have to.

285
00:06:21.927 --> 00:06:23.527
Your competitor will have agents.

286
00:06:23.527 --> 00:06:24.727
So will your employee,

287
00:06:24.727 --> 00:06:26.307
your client, and your suppliers.

288
00:06:26.307 --> 00:06:28.127
And maybe your business will already depend

289
00:06:28.127 --> 00:06:28.507
on them.

290
00:06:28.507 --> 00:06:29.407
At that point,

291
00:06:29.407 --> 00:06:31.027
learning won’t feel relaxed anymore.

292
00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:32.167
It’ll be pressure.

293
00:06:32.267 --> 00:06:33.807
I compare this to learning to drive.

294
00:06:33.807 --> 00:06:35.527
Would you rather learn on a quiet

295
00:06:35.527 --> 00:06:36.887
street or in the middle of a

296
00:06:36.887 --> 00:06:38.587
highway at 75 miles per hour?

297
00:06:38.587 --> 00:06:40.427
Today, we’re on the quiet street.

298
00:06:40.427 --> 00:06:42.047
A street that’s getting less quiet,

299
00:06:42.047 --> 00:06:42.567
it’s true.

300
00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:43.787
But we’re still on it.

301
00:06:43.787 --> 00:06:45.147
And anyone who makes the most of

302
00:06:45.147 --> 00:06:46.987
this period will develop something you won’t

303
00:06:46.987 --> 00:06:48.167
find on any certificate:

304
00:06:48.167 --> 00:06:48.947
judgment.

305
00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:50.427
You look at a problem and immediately

306
00:06:50.427 --> 00:06:52.067
think, “I’d put this with an agent.”

307
00:06:52.067 --> 00:06:53.907
“A person should keep handling this.” “This

308
00:06:53.907 --> 00:06:55.747
agent doesn’t have enough context.” “This one

309
00:06:55.747 --> 00:06:57.647
has too much autonomy.” “And this process

310
00:06:57.647 --> 00:06:59.607
shouldn’t exist at all.” That kind of

311
00:06:59.607 --> 00:07:01.107
insight doesn’t come from watching a lecture

312
00:07:01.107 --> 00:07:01.607
about AI.

313
00:07:01.607 --> 00:07:03.267
It comes from working with agents.

314
00:07:03.267 --> 00:07:04.587
Making mistakes with agents.

315
00:07:04.587 --> 00:07:05.587
Managing agents.

316
00:07:05.587 --> 00:07:06.647
There’s one more thing.

317
00:07:06.647 --> 00:07:08.047
A lot of people think the future

318
00:07:08.047 --> 00:07:09.487
is about knowing how to use the

319
00:07:09.487 --> 00:07:10.027
best tool.

320
00:07:10.027 --> 00:07:11.107
I don’t believe that.

321
00:07:11.107 --> 00:07:12.527
Tools change all the time.

322
00:07:12.527 --> 00:07:13.427
Today it’s one,

323
00:07:13.427 --> 00:07:14.467
tomorrow it’s another.

324
00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:17.207
The skill that lasts is understanding processes.

325
00:07:17.207 --> 00:07:18.147
What comes in.

326
00:07:18.147 --> 00:07:19.007
What happens.

327
00:07:19.007 --> 00:07:19.907
Who decides.

328
00:07:19.907 --> 00:07:20.967
Who does the work.

329
00:07:20.967 --> 00:07:22.347
What result do we expect?

330
00:07:22.347 --> 00:07:23.527
Where are the bottlenecks,

331
00:07:23.527 --> 00:07:25.347
waste, risks, and value?

332
00:07:25.347 --> 00:07:26.767
If you understand the process,

333
00:07:26.767 --> 00:07:27.707
you can put a person,

334
00:07:27.707 --> 00:07:29.107
an agent, an automation,

335
00:07:29.107 --> 00:07:30.327
or all three together in it.

336
00:07:30.327 --> 00:07:32.307
That’s why management is still so important.

337
00:07:32.307 --> 00:07:33.687
Maybe more important than ever.

338
00:07:33.687 --> 00:07:35.687
For decades, managers managed people.

339
00:07:35.687 --> 00:07:37.987
Then they started managing people and systems.

340
00:07:37.987 --> 00:07:39.387
Now they’ll manage people,

341
00:07:39.387 --> 00:07:40.367
systems, and agents.

342
00:07:40.367 --> 00:07:41.607
Maybe dozens of agents.

343
00:07:41.607 --> 00:07:42.327
Maybe hundreds.

344
00:07:42.327 --> 00:07:44.347
And that dramatically changes what one person

345
00:07:44.347 --> 00:07:44.747
can do.

346
00:07:44.747 --> 00:07:46.967
A manager who coordinated ten people might

347
00:07:46.967 --> 00:07:48.807
soon coordinate ten people and fifty agents.

348
00:07:48.807 --> 00:07:50.047
But there’s one condition:

349
00:07:50.047 --> 00:07:51.227
they need to know how to manage

350
00:07:51.227 --> 00:07:52.027
that capacity.

351
00:07:52.027 --> 00:07:54.247
Because a hundred poorly managed agents create

352
00:07:54.247 --> 00:07:55.607
a hundred times more confusion.

353
00:07:55.607 --> 00:07:57.697
Technology increases capacity.

354
00:07:57.697 --> 00:08:00.187
Management is what turns capacity into results.

355
00:08:00.187 --> 00:08:00.987
In the past,

356
00:08:00.987 --> 00:08:02.567
you had value because you knew how

357
00:08:02.567 --> 00:08:03.307
to do the work.

358
00:08:03.307 --> 00:08:04.707
Then, because you knew how to do

359
00:08:04.707 --> 00:08:05.787
it better than the client.

360
00:08:05.787 --> 00:08:07.537
Now the client will be able to

361
00:08:07.537 --> 00:08:08.267
do it too.

362
00:08:08.267 --> 00:08:08.947
So more and more,

363
00:08:08.947 --> 00:08:10.487
your value will come from knowing what

364
00:08:10.487 --> 00:08:11.647
to do, in what order,

365
00:08:11.647 --> 00:08:12.667
why, with whom,

366
00:08:12.667 --> 00:08:13.967
and especially what not to do.

367
00:08:13.967 --> 00:08:15.607
And that’s much harder to automate than

368
00:08:15.607 --> 00:08:16.927
connecting two APIs.

369
00:08:16.927 --> 00:08:18.447
So how do you really get started?

370
00:08:18.447 --> 00:08:20.287
I suggest a simple exercise for this

371
00:08:20.287 --> 00:08:20.607
week.

372
00:08:20.607 --> 00:08:22.027
Pick one of your processes.

373
00:08:22.027 --> 00:08:22.707
Just one.

374
00:08:22.707 --> 00:08:24.687
Something you or your team does every

375
00:08:24.687 --> 00:08:25.247
week.

376
00:08:25.247 --> 00:08:26.947
And answer, in writing,

377
00:08:26.947 --> 00:08:28.527
the same questions you’d ask a new

378
00:08:28.527 --> 00:08:29.247
employee.

379
00:08:29.247 --> 00:08:30.767
What’s the goal of this task?

380
00:08:30.767 --> 00:08:33.047
What context and information are needed?

381
00:08:33.047 --> 00:08:34.467
What tools can be used?

382
00:08:34.467 --> 00:08:36.087
What can be done independently,

383
00:08:36.087 --> 00:08:37.607
and what needs your approval?

384
00:08:37.607 --> 00:08:38.967
And how will you know if it’s

385
00:08:38.967 --> 00:08:39.327
good?

386
00:08:39.327 --> 00:08:40.507
Then give it to an agent and

387
00:08:40.507 --> 00:08:41.187
see what happens.

388
00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:42.187
It’ll get something wrong.

389
00:08:42.187 --> 00:08:43.187
And that’s where you learn.

390
00:08:43.187 --> 00:08:44.267
Adjust the context,

391
00:08:44.267 --> 00:08:45.067
adjust the rule,

392
00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:45.887
and run it again.

393
00:08:45.887 --> 00:08:46.347
That’s it.

394
00:08:46.347 --> 00:08:47.567
You’ve just managed an agent.

395
00:08:47.567 --> 00:08:48.687
That’s why I don’t think we’re nearing

396
00:08:48.687 --> 00:08:49.507
the end of automation.

397
00:08:49.507 --> 00:08:50.707
We’re nearing the end of a phase.

398
00:08:50.707 --> 00:08:51.987
The phase when knowing how to connect

399
00:08:51.987 --> 00:08:53.387
tools was enough to make you an

400
00:08:53.387 --> 00:08:53.767
expert.

401
00:08:53.767 --> 00:08:55.707
Now the management phase is beginning.

402
00:08:55.707 --> 00:08:56.947
Managing processes.

403
00:08:56.947 --> 00:08:57.367
People.

404
00:08:57.367 --> 00:08:58.147
Agents.

405
00:08:58.147 --> 00:08:59.067
Capacity.

406
00:08:59.067 --> 00:09:00.527
Agents will keep improving.

407
00:09:00.527 --> 00:09:02.107
Models will keep getting cheaper.

408
00:09:02.107 --> 00:09:03.067
You can’t control that.

409
00:09:03.067 --> 00:09:04.987
What you can control is how well

410
00:09:04.987 --> 00:09:07.247
you understand this new environment before it

411
00:09:07.247 --> 00:09:08.147
becomes mandatory.

412
00:09:08.147 --> 00:09:10.167
So don’t just watch videos about agents.

413
00:09:10.167 --> 00:09:11.087
Use agents.

414
00:09:11.087 --> 00:09:12.547
Don’t just read about the terminal.

415
00:09:12.547 --> 00:09:13.587
Open the terminal.

416
00:09:13.587 --> 00:09:15.567
Don’t just hear people talk about VPSs.

417
00:09:15.567 --> 00:09:16.467
Set one up.

418
00:09:16.467 --> 00:09:17.907
And don’t wait for your company to

419
00:09:17.907 --> 00:09:18.687
make you learn.

420
00:09:18.687 --> 00:09:20.087
Learn while you still have time to

421
00:09:20.087 --> 00:09:20.807
make mistakes.

422
00:09:20.807 --> 00:09:22.847
Because when everyone realizes they need to,

423
00:09:22.847 --> 00:09:24.367
the advantage will belong to those who

424
00:09:24.367 --> 00:09:25.227
started earlier.

425
00:09:25.227 --> 00:09:26.907
The manager of the future won’t just

426
00:09:26.907 --> 00:09:27.567
manage people.

427
00:09:27.567 --> 00:09:28.567
They’ll manage people,

428
00:09:28.567 --> 00:09:29.607
processes, and agents.

429
00:09:29.607 --> 00:09:31.347
And that future isn’t far away.

430
00:09:31.347 --> 00:09:32.587
It’s already begun.

